WEBVTT
TITLE View Serialisability and Blind Writes: the Read-From Table, the Gap Above Conflict Serialisability, and the Four-Class Ladder — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Three transactions touch one item: T1 reads X then writes it, T2 writes X, T3 writes X.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The precedence graph has two edges in opposite directions: T1 to T2, and T2 to T1.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
So it is not conflict serialisable. Run it with X at 100: read 100, write 150, write 200, write 150, write 300.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Serial T1 then T2 then T3 also ends at 300 with T1 reading the initial value, so the schedule is equivalent to a serial one.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A schedule leaves behind three things: who read the initial value, who each read took its value from, and who wrote last.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
In the witness: T1 read the initial value, and T3 wrote the final value.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The 200 that T2 wrote was never read by anyone. A write nobody reads is called dead.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Those three observations are the view of the schedule, and a dead write can move without changing it.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Two schedules are view equivalent when all three observations agree.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Add a transaction T_init that writes every item at the start, and T_final that reads every item at the end.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
View equivalent exactly when the read-from relation is identical. View serialisable when some serial order matches it.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The witness has two rows: T1 read X from T_init, and T_final read X from T3.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
X starts at 100. T1 reads 100.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
T2 writes 200 into X.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
T1 writes 150, computed from the 100 it read. The 200 is now dead.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
T3 writes 300. Final value 300, and serial T1, T2, T3 produces the same three observations.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Three transactions give six serial orders. Compare the read-from table with each one.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The last transaction in a serial order writes last, so the final-write row names it immediately.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Only T1, T2, T3 has T1 reading the initial value and T3 writing last. That is the match.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
A match is enough. The definition asks for one serial order, not for the order the operations look like.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Four operations with two from T1 and one each from T2 and T3 give twelve interleavings.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Six are conflict serialisable, and here they are exactly the six serial orders.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Ten are view serialisable, so four have a cycle and are still correct.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The other two are in neither class: T1 writes last but computed from the initial value.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A blind write is a write with no earlier read of the same item in its own transaction.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Two larger families: 210 interleavings give 54 conflict and 71 view; 560 give 86 and 110.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Across the three families, 45 schedules sit in the gap, and every one contains a blind write.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
With no blind write at all the two classes coincide: 90 interleavings, 30 and 30.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Three two-transaction families: 3, 6 and 20 interleavings.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
In each family the two counts agree: 2 of 3, 2 of 6, 8 of 20.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
29 schedules, no separation. This page reports the enumeration; it does not prove the general case.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The exam consequence: a view-serialisability question needs a blind write, and in practice three transactions.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Four classes, each strictly inside the one before: serial, conflict, view, final-state.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Witnesses: r1 w1 w2 r2 of Y w3 of X w2 of Y w3 of Y is conflict but not serial; r1 w2 w1 w3 is view but not conflict; r1 r2 w1 w2 is final-state but not view.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Testing conflict serialisability is a cycle check. Testing view serialisability is NP-complete.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Locking cannot produce every view-serialisable schedule, so real systems aim at conflict serialisability.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: write the schedule with one row per transaction, in execution order.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: mark every write that has no earlier read of the same item in its own row.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: with no blind write, the precedence graph decides both questions at once.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step four: otherwise build the read-from table and compare it with each serial order. Report the two verdicts separately.

